tion events in favor of social cohesion.", "The checklist is so granular that it guides the user to the answer without requiring any active cognitive reconciliation."], "typicality": 0.7, "sidecar": {"requested_context_aliases": [], "search_signal": "productive"}, "optional_refs": ["SRC_006"]}, {"claim": "Expert intuition will remain superior for tasks where the 'verifiable outcome' is an emergent property of complex interactions, whereas checklists only succeed in additive verification tasks.", "mechanism": "Intuition in domain experts is actually compressed high-dimensional pattern matching. In complex systems, the 'right answer' depends on the interaction between variables. A checklist decomposes the problem into independent atoms (linearization). If the outcome is non-linear, the checklist-following panel will reliably identify all atoms but fail to synthesize the emergent property, whereas the expert's intuition captures the gestalt structure of the problem.", "counterconditions": ["The 'complex' task can be perfectly decomposed into a finite set of binary checks without loss of signal.", "The expert's intuition is corrupted by outdated mental models (cognitive fossilization)."], "typicality": 0.5, "sidecar": {"requested_context_aliases": [], "search_signal": "productive"}, "optional_refs": ["SRC_007"]}], "scratch_proposal": {"new_blocks": [{"body": {"content": "If we treat the checklist as a 'distributed memory' and the panel as 'distributed processing', the reliability gain is essentially a reduction in the variance of the output. The expert has low variance but may be centered on a biased mean. The panel has high internal variance but the checklist centers their collective mean on the objective truth. The 'distinguishing evidence' is whether the final answer is a result of variance-reduction (convergence) or simply a high-probability guess by the most dominant panel member.", "possible_next_move": "Formalize the variance-reduction hypothesis as a simulation 